<title>FRANCE: Total 1996 net income rises strongly.</title>
<headline>Total 1996 net income rises strongly.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-01-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year to December 31, 1996.</p>
<p>(Figures in billions of French francs unless stated)</p>
<p>    Net income after special items	   5.6  vs	     2.2</p>
<p>    Net income before special items	  5.6  vs	     3.7</p>
<p>    Earnings per share (franc)		23.3  vs	    15.8</p>
<p>    Operating income			    10.2  vs	     7.4</p>
<p>    Exceptional				   nil   vs charge    1.5</p>
<p>    Cashflow					15.2  vs	    11.3</p>
<p>    Investments				     16  vs	    12.1</p>
<p>    Divestments				    2.6  vs	     2.3</p>
<p>    Consolidated sales			   176  vs	   136.8</p>
<p>    Debt-equity ratio (percent)		 19  vs		18</p>
<p>Breakdown of operating income</p>
<p>    -- Upstream				    7.3  vs	     4.4</p>
<p>    -- Downstream				  1.5  vs	     1.3</p>
<p>    -- Chemicals				   1.9  vs	     1.7</p>
<p>NOTE - Total SA gave the estimated profit figures in a statement. Financial analysts, polled by Jacques Chahine Finance were expecting an average earnings per share figure of 23.30 francs.</p>
<p>Total took 1.5 billion francs in special charges for a change to new U.S. accounting standards and a restructuring programme in 1995.</p>
<p>This year it took 500 million francs in charges for the Lylle-Frigg platform offshore of Norway, but the charge was taken from the operating income and not as an exceptional charge.</p>
</text>
